\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Convention}
\title{Clifford generators and polarized operators}

\p{Let #{Q} be a quadratic form on a module #{V} over a commutative ring #{K}.
Write

##{B_Q(u,v)=Q(u+v)-Q(u)-Q(v)}

for its unhalved polar form, and reserve #{\iota_Q:V\to\Cl(Q)} for the
canonical Clifford generator map. Then

##{\iota_Q(u)\iota_Q(v)+\iota_Q(v)\iota_Q(u)
=B_Q(u,v)1.}

If #{2} is invertible, write #{b_Q=\tfrac12B_Q}; the same relation is then
#{\{\iota_Q(u),\iota_Q(v)\}=2b_Q(u,v)1}. No use of #{b_Q} is made when
division by two is unavailable.}

\p{For polarization data #{V\simeq(W\oplus W')\oplus L} and
#{S=\bigwedge_K W}, the definitions are in \ref{fcap-001I}. Write
#{\varepsilon_x:S\to S} for creation,
#{a_y=\iota_{\varphi_y}:S\to S} for contraction,
#{\alpha:S\to S} for parity, #{\lambda_z:S\to S} for the orthogonal-line
action, and #{c(v):S\to S} for the assembled Clifford operator.

In particular,

##{a_y\varepsilon_x+\varepsilon_xa_y
=B_Q(x,y)\operatorname{id}_S.}

This is the normalization of
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.contract_wedge}; the assembled
square #{c(v)^2=Q(v)\operatorname{id}_S} is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.cliffordOperator_sq}. The
contracted notation #{\iota_y} in \ref{fcap-001J} and \ref{fcap-001P} is a
legacy alias for #{a_y=\iota_{\varphi_y}}; it does not denote the generator
map #{\iota_Q}.}

\p{The source conventions translate as follows. Fulton--Harris use a
symmetric bilinear form #{Q_{\mathrm{FH}}} and write the anticommutator as
#{2Q_{\mathrm{FH}}}; their contraction functional is also
#{2Q_{\mathrm{FH}}(-,w')}
\citet{Section 20.1, (20.1), Lemma 20.9 and (20.11), pp. 301, 304--305}{fulton2004representation}.
Meinrenken likewise starts from #{B} with Clifford relation #{2B} and, after
choosing #{V=F^*\oplus F}, acts on #{\bigwedge F^*} by creation from #{F^*}
and contraction from #{F}
\citet{Section 3.2.1--3.2.2, Proposition 3.5, pp. 55--57}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
Thus #{Q_{\mathrm{FH}}=B=b_Q} whenever #{b_Q} exists. Goodman--Wallach
instead name the anticommutator form #{\beta}; under their identification
#{W_{\mathrm{GW}}^*=W} and #{W_{\mathrm{GW}}=W'}, one has
#{\beta=B_Q}
\citet{Definition 6.1.1 and Theorem 6.1.3, proof, pp. 302, 304--305}{goodman2009symmetry}.
The formulas agree after these substitutions; the different letters do not
signal different Clifford actions.}
